  "body": {
    "id": "lem:compact-moving-remainder-covariance",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Under \\(\\text{ass:multinomial-interior}\\), \\(\\text{ass:multinomial-base-convergence}\\), and \\(\\text{ass:multinomial-local-expansion}\\), for every rational compact polytope \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\),\n\\[\n \\max_{j\\in\\mathcal J_0^{\\mathrm{act}}}\\sup_{u\\in C}|e_{n,j}(u)|\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nwith the maximum interpreted as zero when \\(\\mathcal J_0^{\\mathrm{act}}=\\varnothing\\), and\n\\[\n \\left[B'\\operatorname{diag}\\{p_n(0)\\}^{-1}B\\right]^{-1}\\longrightarrow\n \\Sigma_0.\n\\]",
    "free_symbols": [
      "eSlackN",
      "JactiveZero",
      "u",
      "d",
      "n",
      "pn",
      "pZero",
      "Bmap",
      "SigmaZero",
      "IZero",
      "kappa"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:multinomial-interior",
      "ass:multinomial-base-convergence",
      "ass:multinomial-local-expansion",
      "def:moving-slack-system"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Write \\(\\psi_j(p)=\\alpha_j'p+\\beta_j\\).  Affinity and the definition of the moving remainder give the exact identity\n\\[\n e_{n,j}(u)=\\alpha_j'\n \\left(\\sqrt n[\\{p_n(u)-p_n(0)\\}]-Bu\\right).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\mathcal J_0^{\\mathrm{act}}\\) is finite, Cauchy--Schwarz implies, for every rational compact polytope \\(C\\),\n\\[\n \\max_j\\sup_{u\\in C}|e_{n,j}(u)|\n \\leq\\left(\\max_j\\|\\alpha_j\\|_2\\right)\n \\sup_{u\\in C}\\left\\|\\sqrt n[\\{p_n(u)-p_n(0)\\}]-Bu\\right\\|_2\n \\longrightarrow0\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nby the compact-uniform local-expansion assumption.  When the active set is empty, the maximum is zero by convention.\n\nBase convergence and \\(\\min_j(p_0)_j\\geq\\kappa>0\\) imply that every coordinate of \\(p_n(0)\\) is eventually at least \\(\\kappa/2\\).  Coordinatewise reciprocal is continuous on that set, and therefore\n\\[\n B'\\operatorname{diag}\\{p_n(0)\\}^{-1}B\n \\longrightarrow B'\\operatorname{diag}(p_0)^{-1}B=I_0.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\ne0\\), full column rank of \\(B\\) and positivity of \\(p_0\\) give\n\\[\n x'I_0x=\\|\\operatorname{diag}(p_0)^{-1/2}Bx\\|_2^2>0.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nThus \\(I_0\\) is positive definite.  Matrix inversion is continuous on the positive-definite cone, so (3)--(4) and \\(\\Sigma_0=I_0^{-1}\\) yield\n\\[\n [B'\\operatorname{diag}\\{p_n(0)\\}^{-1}B]^{-1}\\longrightarrow\\Sigma_0.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]",
